
  Two harms from one line: the client gets a [38;5;153m201[39m with an id for an order that was never persisted, and on Node ≥15 the
  unhandled rejection kills the process — one malformed request takes down the service. Root cause is an error-channel
  mismatch: the handler reports errors by [3mreturning[0m [38;5;153m{status: 400}[39m, the store by [3mrejecting[0m.

  [1mWhy CI didn't catch either[0m

  The suite passes (2/2). [38;5;153mtest/handlers.test.js:25-27[39m asserts [38;5;153mres.orders.length === 10[39m, which holds for pages 0, 1, and
  2 alike — it can't distinguish correct pagination from an off-by-one. And the only creation test exercises the
  [3mrejection[0m branch, returning before [38;5;153msaveOrder[39m is ever called, so C2 has no coverage at all. Fixing the code without
  tightening these assertions leaves the same gap open.

  [1mWorth doing, not blocking[0m

  [38;5;153msrc/handlers.js:15[39m — [38;5;153mlistOrdersHandler()[39m with no args now throws; [38;5;153mquery = {}[39m restores it. [38;5;153msrc/handlers.js:17[39m — [38;5;153msize[39m is
